WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.140
- Hey Legal Eagles!

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.740
It's time to adjudicate the matter

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:05.861
of Judge Kavanaugh vs.
Dr. Christine Blasey Ford.

00:00:05.861 --> 00:00:08.944
(simple tonal music)

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:12.990
All right, let's think like a lawyer.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:13.950
Like most of the country,

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.890
I've been glued to the
Kavanaugh/Ford hearings.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.850
Regardless of what side
of the aisle you stand,

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.940
I think it raises important
questions for our country.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.160
It also raises particular legal issues

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.480
that I think deserve a thorough debate.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.110
So, today I'd like to give
you the first real law review

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:31.530
of the Kavanaugh/Ford hearing

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.870
from the perspective of a lawyer.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.010
This will be the first in a
new series on this channel

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:36.843
where I try to tackle

00:00:36.843 --> 00:00:39.470
the most important
legal issues of the day.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:40.630
If you have a suggestion

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:42.280
for the next legal topic I should cover,

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:43.830
please leave that down below

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:44.920
and if you disagree with me,

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.930
be sure to leave your comment
in the form of an objection.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:49.370
And, by the way, if you'd like to see me

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:51.120
critique your favorite legal TV show,

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.410
like Suits or Better Call Saul,

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:55.120
check out my Real Lawyer Reacts series,

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.470
the link for that will be down below.

00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:01.070
Now, there are five questions
that I want to cover today.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:04.990
Number one: Did Judge
Kavanaugh commit legal perjury?

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:08.530
Number two: Did he display
a judge-like temperament?

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.830
Number three: Would
Judge Kavanaugh prevent

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.540
investigations into President
Trump or the White House?

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.910
Number four: Would the current allegations

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:18.860
of sexual assault hold up in court?

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.890
And number five: Should Judge
Kavanaugh have requested

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:24.540
an investigation by the FBI?

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.410
Now, before we begin, on a personal note,

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:28.620
this is not meant to
be a partisan analysis.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.880
I'm not gonna give you
my opinion on whether

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.080
I think Judge Kavanaugh should be elevated

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.390
to the Supreme Court based
on his judicial philosophy

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:37.920
or his political views.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.950
I'm a lawyer first and
that's the perspective

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:41.780
I'm going to try to give you.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:43.290
Oh, and one last thing.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.860
This is Stella the Legal Beagle.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.680
She reads all of your comments.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.610
Now, I want this to be a hearty debate,

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.490
but I want it to be a civil debate.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:53.370
If you wouldn't say it in front of Stella,

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.910
please reword it so that
it's Stella-appropriate.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.540
All right, so with that out
of the way, let's begin.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:02.040
So, question number one, did
Judge Kavanaugh commit perjury?

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.260
Now, perjury is a very specific crime.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.810
Taken from Title 18 of the US Code,

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:10.950
perjury is defined as having taken an oath

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:14.180
before a competent tribunal
that he will testify truly

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.990
and contrary to such
oath states or subscribes

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:20.150
any material matter which he
does not believe to be true.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:23.060
Now, looking at a lot of the
footage from the hearing,

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.640
it seems to me that a lot
of Judge Kavanaugh's answers

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.990
appeared to be evasive and non-responsive.

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:31.530
For example, Judge Kavanaugh was asked

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:34.980
what ralphing referred to
in the context of Beach Week

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.810
and he defined as referring to

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:40.560
his chronically weak stomach
from eating spicy food

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.053
and that he can't really
keep his food down.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.790
- Let's look at Beach Week
Ralph Club Biggest Contributor,

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:51.123
what does the word ralph mean in that?

00:02:51.123 --> 00:02:54.000
- That probably refers to throwing up,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.390
I'm known to have a weak
stomach and I always have.

00:02:56.390 --> 00:02:57.720
In fact, the last time I was here,

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.630
you asked me about having
ketchup on spaghetti,

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.600
I always have had a weak stomach.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:05.480
- Judge Kavanaugh was asked
to define Devil's Triangle,

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.510
as it appeared in his yearbook.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.240
He defined Devil's
Triangle as a drinking game

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.080
and not any kind of sexual act.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:14.751
- Devil's Triangle?

00:03:14.751 --> 00:03:18.010
- (sniffles) Drinking game.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:19.570
- How's it played?

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:22.720
- Three glasses in a triangle.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:23.553
- And?

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:27.140
- You ever played Quarters?

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:27.973
- Nope.

00:03:27.973 --> 00:03:29.960
- Okay, it's a Quarters game.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.290
- Judge Kavanaugh was also asked to define

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:35.520
Renate alumnus as it
appears in his yearbook.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:39.310
He explains that that
refers to being friends

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:42.720
with a woman who went to
a neighboring high school.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.310
- You mentioned, I think, the
Renate, or Renate, Renata,

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:47.930
I don't know how you pronounce that,

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:50.370
that's a proper name of
an individual you know?

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:51.280
- Renate.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:52.310
- Renate.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.930
It's spelled with an E at the
end, R-E-N-A-T-E, is that--

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:55.763
- Correct.

00:03:55.763 --> 00:03:56.596
- Okay.

00:03:56.596 --> 00:03:58.980
And then after that is
the word "alumnius."

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.440
What does the word "alumnius"
mean in that context?

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.450
- I explained that in
my opening statement.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:06.413
We, she was a great friend of ours.

00:04:07.807 --> 00:04:09.070
A bunch of us went to dances with her,

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:10.690
she hung out with us as a group.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.700
The media circus that has
been generated by this thought

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:17.970
and reported that it
referred to sex, it did not.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:20.930
- Now, given that I was
once a 17-year-old boy,

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:22.420
it seems to me that a lot of these answers

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:26.020
are evasive at best and
flat-out wrong in the worst case

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.440
and that a lot of these
terms simply don't jive

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.540
with the common understanding
of a lot of the way

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.630
that these words would be used.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.190
Some seem unrealistic.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:40.250
But perjury requires a
very specific mens rea

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:43.760
that you knowingly state something
that is false under oath.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.620
It's possible that he and his friends

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:49.010
had very idiosyncratic definitions
of some of these words,

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:50.960
perhaps they were taken out of context,

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.410
and on top of that, it would
be very difficult to find

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:57.850
a definitive guide on what
these words actually mean.

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:00.040
Language is always changing.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:01.850
But I think a lot of the problems stem

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:04.840
from the Democratic
senators failing to pin

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.190
Judge Kavanaugh down to
an actual definition.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:11.810
If you were to think of this
hearing like a deposition,

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.210
the point is not necessarily to argue

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:16.200
or get a Perry Mason-like admission,

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:20.270
but to lock down the witness
into a specific story

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:22.160
and a specific definition.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.800
You lock them down so they can't back out

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:26.390
and you can go down the rabbit hole

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:28.120
to show how ridiculous it is.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.520
Now, normally you don't
ask the witness questions

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:32.110
you don't know the answer to,

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:34.150
but if you know that the answers

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:36.540
are going to be fairly ridiculous,

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:39.310
then you want the witness to explain

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:40.910
all of the repercussions that come

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:43.680
from the idiosyncratic or ridiculous view

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:45.940
that they're taking and
so you wanna take them

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:48.300
down that road and take it to an extreme

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.920
to show how ridiculous it seems.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.950
Here, a lot of the Democratic senators

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:53.910
failed to ask follow-up questions

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:56.050
that would've pinned down Judge Kavanaugh.

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:57.640
For example, when he refers

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:59.750
to the Devil's Triangle
as a drinking game,

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:01.260
you could really pin him down

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.590
to show how ridiculous that is.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:04.627
You could say that, "It's your testimony

00:06:04.627 --> 00:06:08.210
"that the Devil's Triangle
is a drinking game, correct?"

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:09.697
You get a yes answer.

00:06:09.697 --> 00:06:12.087
"It's your testimony
that the Devil's Triangle

00:06:12.087 --> 00:06:15.670
"does not refer to a
sexual act of some kind?"

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:17.257
He'll probably answer no.

00:06:17.257 --> 00:06:19.537
"It's your testimony
that the Devil's Triangle

00:06:19.537 --> 00:06:21.517
"does not refer to a sexual act

00:06:21.517 --> 00:06:23.780
"with one woman and two men?"

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:25.560
He'll probably answer no again.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.110
The senators could've followed
up about his weak stomach.

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:29.330
They could've asked if he throws up

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:31.220
every time he has spicy food.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.430
They could've asked when was the last time

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:36.070
he had Indian food, when was the last time

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:38.220
he had Mexican food, Cajun food,

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:39.210
and just go down the line.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:41.870
And, granted, they might not
be able to get him to admit

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:45.890
that maybe he has spicy food
more often than he lets on

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:48.200
and maybe he doesn't vomit
every time he has it,

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:50.900
and that the vomiting
probably relates to drinking

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:54.040
more than it does to having a
quote-unquote "weak stomach,"

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.330
but what they can do is lay the foundation

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:59.990
to catch him in falsehoods later on.

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:03.490
That's one of the big issues
with this particular hearing

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:06.580
is that a lot of the
senators were trying to argue

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:09.040
at the same time they were
trying to ask questions

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:11.210
and as any trial lawyer will tell you,

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:15.070
you can not argue at the same
time you're asking questions.

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:18.000
Those are two separate functions
that need to be separated.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.430
So the Democratic senators
should have done a better job

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:23.780
of separating the cross-examinations
from the arguments

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:25.710
to do a better job of
laying the foundation

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:28.590
for catching Judge Kavanaugh
in outright falsehoods.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:31.700
As in court, one's character
for telling the truth

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:34.760
on the stand is almost always relevant

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:36.130
and if you lay the foundation,

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:38.770
just getting out facts
that don't seem reasonable,

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:41.180
then perhaps after the hearing is finished

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:42.760
you can go do some investigations

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.720
and find evidence that contradicts

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.010
what Judge Kavanaugh stated on the stand

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:49.850
and I suspect if they had asked
better follow-up questions,

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:51.350
they would've been able to do that.

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:53.720
And if we're thinking about
these particular questions

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:55.100
as a cross-examination,

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.340
like what would happen in
trial, a lot of the questioners

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:01.450
allowed the witness to evade the question.

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:04.120
They fell for the witness' obfuscation

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:06.270
and abandoned their original question.

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:08.420
And as any ligator will tell you,

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:11.590
if you don't get an answer to
the question that you asked,

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:14.470
you ask that exact question again

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:16.480
and you ask that exact question

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:18.960
until you get the answer
that you're looking for.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.257
So, for example, when
Judge Kavanaugh was asked,

00:08:21.257 --> 00:08:24.610
"Would you want Mark Judge
to testify on your behalf?"

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.750
The Senator asked the same question once,

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:28.460
but then he abandons that question

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:30.150
to then argue with the witness,

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:34.080
falling for the trap of
wasting his time on argument

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:36.970
instead of following up
with the exact question.

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:39.593
- If she's saying Mark
Judge was in the room then,

00:08:39.593 --> 00:08:42.073
then he should be in the room here today.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:47.883
Would you want him called as a witness?

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:53.450
- Senator, this allegation
came into the committee--

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:55.470
- Oh no, no, I'm just asking the question,

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:58.110
would you want him to
be here as a witness?

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:00.820
- He's already provided sworn
testimony to the committee.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:03.450
This allegation's been
hidden by the committee--

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:06.030
- No, it's hasn't been--
- by members of the committee.

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:08.620
- It has not been investigated by the FBI,

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.660
the committee has refused
to allow it to be--

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:13.040
- It was dropped on me, it was sprung--

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:15.260
- It was not investigated by the FBI

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:18.130
and he was not been called
where he might be under oath--

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:20.730
- Shoulda been handled in
the due course, senator,

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:22.020
when it came in.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:23.995
- I would disagree with that.

00:09:23.995 --> 00:09:26.140
I've been on this committee 44 years.

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:28.320
- I think if the senator had asked

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.970
the same question again
in the correct way,

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:34.000
I think he would've
gotten a yes or no answer.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.070
But the fact is, you can't argue

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:38.230
and question at the same time.

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:40.130
If the witness is non-responsive,

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:43.340
you have to stop the testimony
and make them answer.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:45.149
Now, in trial, you have the advantage

00:09:45.149 --> 00:09:49.160
of having the judge there to
force the witness to answer.

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:51.470
Here, of course, there is no neutral judge

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:53.190
to force the witness to answer,

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:54.990
you can't object as non-responsive

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:58.410
and get the judge to step in
and make the witness answer.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:00.470
And the witness here, Judge Kavanaugh,

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:03.050
has every incentive to waste
as much time as possible

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:05.060
knowing that the senators only have

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:07.290
five minutes for each person.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:10.330
Despite the witness having
every incentive to waste time,

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:12.560
there are plenty of
tactics that you can use

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:14.420
to get the witness to answer yes or no.

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:17.330
Now, first of all, you have
to ask a yes or a no question.

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:20.140
This doesn't work unless you
ask very pointed questions,

00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:21.940
which a lot of the senators failed to do.

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:24.450
But, if you are phrasing
your questions correctly

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:26.090
in the form of a yes or no question,

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:27.900
and the witness is still non-responsive,

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:29.670
here's an example of what you can do.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:31.837
You can say, "Thank you for that answer,

00:10:31.837 --> 00:10:33.367
"but my question was different.

00:10:33.367 --> 00:10:36.160
"My question was," and then
just repeat your question.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:39.110
And then if the witness
is still non-responsive,

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:40.210
then you escalate it.

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:41.327
You interrupt the witness and say,

00:10:41.327 --> 00:10:43.477
"I'm sorry, the question that I asked was,

00:10:43.477 --> 00:10:46.670
"yes or no," blank and
then reask the question.

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:48.350
Now, if you have more time,

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:50.260
then you can try and get
the witness on your side

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:52.440
by explaining that you're trying

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:54.290
to get an answer to your question

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:56.880
and that the other side
will have the opportunity

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:59.550
to allow you to expound on your answer,

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:01.860
but you're just asking
for a yes or no question.

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:03.100
So, I don't want this to seem

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:05.750
like it should just be an
incredibly aggressive process,

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:07.520
it sort of depends on the witness,

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:09.820
but there are a ton of rhetorical devices

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:12.880
that you can use to
get yes or no responses

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:15.060
to accurately phrased questions.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:16.690
And here, unfortunately,

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:18.340
I don't think a lot of
the Democratic senators

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:20.530
had much trial experience

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:23.410
and didn't have a lot of
the rhetorical devices

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:25.010
in their pocket that they should have.

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:27.240
All right, so let's think like a lawyer

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:28.500
in regards to this question.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.070
In other words, let's try to come up

00:11:30.070 --> 00:11:33.340
with the strongest arguments
on both sides of this issue.

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:34.820
I think the Democrats would argue

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:37.850
that these answers don't comport

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:40.060
with anyone's reasonable expectation

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:43.560
of these particular words
and the only explanation

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:46.910
is that Judge Kavanaugh is
trying to avoid the truth

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:48.900
in order to get through this hearing.

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:50.660
They don't comport with common experience

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:53.770
and they are not accurate
representations of these words.

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:55.800
I think that the Republican response

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:57.470
would be that the Democratic senators

00:11:57.470 --> 00:12:00.300
failed to ask good
cross-examination questions,

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:01.810
they failed to pin down the witness,

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:05.270
they allowed the witness
to evade the questions

00:12:05.270 --> 00:12:07.750
and give the answers
that he wanted to give

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:09.690
and failed to pin down the witness

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:11.570
to a particular answer that qualifies

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:13.970
as the legal definition of perjury.

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:15.450
Now, there is a separate question

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:18.640
as to whether a nominee
should evade questions

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:22.000
and obfuscate, but that brings
us to our second question,

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:26.110
did Judge Kavanaugh display
a judge-like temperament?

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:29.870
Now, any judge holds a
special position in society.

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:31.920
They are the arbiters of our disputes.

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:34.370
Theoretically, they
should all be impartial.

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:36.210
They need to be able to hear both sides

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:37.410
and make judgment calls

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:41.050
that have a huge effect
on the litigants' lives.

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:44.530
Now, Justices of the Supreme
Court are appointed for life

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:46.990
and they hold an even
higher place in our society,

00:12:46.990 --> 00:12:50.520
they literally make life or
death decisions sometimes.

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:53.840
So, it's not surprising that
both judges and justices

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:56.870
are held to a very, very high standard.

00:12:56.870 --> 00:12:58.840
They are supposed to be neutral,

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:01.700
they are not supposed to
take a partisan stance,

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:03.330
they are supposed to be able to be

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:06.250
the neutral arbiters of
our general disputes.

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:07.890
In his opening statement,

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:10.510
Judge Kavanaugh made some
very provocative statements

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:12.280
about the backlash being

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:16.000
a result of revenge from the Clintons.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:19.330
- This whole two week
effort has been a calculated

00:13:19.330 --> 00:13:22.400
and orchestrated political hit,

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:24.670
fueled with apparent pent up anger

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:28.673
about President Trump
and the 2016 election,

00:13:30.030 --> 00:13:34.730
fear that has been unfairly
stoked about my judicial record,

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:36.983
revenge on behalf of the Clintons,

00:13:37.870 --> 00:13:40.930
and millions of dollars
in money from outside,

00:13:40.930 --> 00:13:43.493
left-wing opposition groups.

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:47.120
This is a circus.

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:49.420
- Judge Kavanaugh also
got into a sparing match

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:53.340
with Senator Klobuchar when
she asked about past drinking.

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:57.140
- I think you've probably
had beers, Senator, and--

00:13:57.140 --> 00:13:59.450
- So you're saying
there's never been a case

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:02.310
where you drank so much
that you didn't remember

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:04.420
what happened the night before
or part of what happened?

00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:06.400
- It's, you're asking
about, yeah, black out,

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:07.793
I don't know, have you?

00:14:09.738 --> 00:14:11.940
- Could you answer the question, Judge?

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:15.440
I just, so have, that's not happened?

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:16.600
Is that your answer?

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:19.256
- Yeah and I'm curious if you have.

00:14:19.256 --> 00:14:21.220
- (chuckles) I have no
drinking problem, Judge.

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:22.280
- Yeah, nor do I.

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:23.960
- Okay, thank you.

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:27.020
- And Judge Kavanaugh also
got into some sparing matches

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:29.890
with other senators about
some pointed questions.

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:32.677
- Did the word ralph, you
used in your yearbook--

00:14:32.677 --> 00:14:34.750
- I already said, I already
answered the question.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:36.130
If you're, yeah--

00:14:36.130 --> 00:14:37.660
- Did it relate to alcohol--

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:38.680
- I like beer.
- You haven't answered that.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:39.513
- I like beer.

00:14:39.513 --> 00:14:40.346
- I don't know if you do--
- Okay.

00:14:40.346 --> 00:14:41.780
- Do you like beer, Senator, or not?

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:43.670
What do you like to drink?
- Next one is--

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:44.620
- Senator, what do you like to drink?

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:46.330
- Judge, have you--

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:48.270
- So, let's think like a lawyer.

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:51.290
I think the Democrats would probably argue

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:55.640
that this shows a character
for not being impartial,

00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:59.770
for taking partisan stances,
and that his hot-headedness

00:14:59.770 --> 00:15:01.710
shows that he doesn't have the temperament

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:03.430
to be a Supreme Court Justice,

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:05.680
who we hold to a very, very high standard.

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:08.770
Not to say anything of the
allegations of sexual assault

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:11.730
that have given rise
to this whole hearing.

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:14.410
I think the Republicans
would probably respond

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:15.970
that he should not have been

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:17.630
asked these questions in the first place,

00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:20.090
that they weren't proper to begin with,

00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:22.770
and that he is understandably angry

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:24.460
for being put in this position.

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:28.450
Now, I think we can make another
parallel to some trial law.

00:15:28.450 --> 00:15:31.280
As a rule of evidence,
generally character evidence

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:33.880
is not admissible at trial,

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:36.540
but the main exception
is where the accused

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:38.430
has made character an issue

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:39.970
and they usually do that by calling

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:41.320
a bunch of witnesses to explain

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:42.600
how good of a person they are

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:46.090
and how they never could have
done the underlying crime.

00:15:46.090 --> 00:15:49.060
Now, here Judge Kavanaugh
has done something similar,

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:51.350
he has essentially said that
he was not a heavy drinker,

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:52.930
he has never blacked out,

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:54.760
he has never used sexual slang

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:57.010
in the context that he's alleged,

00:15:57.010 --> 00:16:00.240
and he has never engaged in
inappropriate sexual behavior.

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:04.090
In some ways, if we were to
draw a parallel to a trial,

00:16:04.090 --> 00:16:05.620
this has opened the door

00:16:05.620 --> 00:16:08.300
to test his veracity for truthfulness

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:10.930
and also to show whether
the superlative statements

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:13.890
that he is making are true or not.

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:15.500
So, do you think that Judge Kavanaugh

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:17.500
displayed a judge-like behavior?

00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:19.430
Let me know, I leave it to you.

00:16:19.430 --> 00:16:22.410
Now, that takes me to issue number three,

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:25.430
would Judge Kavanaugh, if
elevated to the Supreme Court,

00:16:25.430 --> 00:16:26.810
prevent an investigation

00:16:26.810 --> 00:16:29.170
into the president or the White House?

00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:31.300
Now, a lot of pundits have argued

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:33.040
that Judge Kavanaugh, in particular,

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:34.920
was nominated to the Supreme Court

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:37.330
because he holds an idiosyncratic view

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:38.610
that the sitting president

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:42.140
can not be indicted civilly or criminally.

00:16:42.140 --> 00:16:44.900
A lot of this stems from
a law review article

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:46.920
that he wrote in the Minnesota Law Review

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:48.380
called the Separation of Powers

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:51.240
During the Forty-Fourth
Presidency and Beyond.

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:52.890
In that law review piece,

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:56.080
Judge Kavanaugh argued that
Congress should pass a law

00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:57.850
that while the president is in office,

00:16:57.850 --> 00:17:00.340
he should not be subject to civil lawsuits

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:02.970
or to criminal investigation
or prosecution.

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:05.460
He explained that based on
his first-hand experience

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:07.410
in the White House, he came to believe

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:09.023
that the job of the president is,

00:17:09.023 --> 00:17:11.407
"far more difficult than any other

00:17:11.407 --> 00:17:14.046
"civil position in government."

00:17:14.046 --> 00:17:15.480
And he also says that the president

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:18.630
must be able to focus on
the tasks of being president

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:22.510
and shouldn't be distracted
by civil or criminal trials.

00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:25.150
Judge Kavanaugh recognized
the irony of his position,

00:17:25.150 --> 00:17:27.790
given that he started his legal career

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:30.440
in the Independent Counsel's
Office with Kenneth Starr

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:33.130
during the Clinton
impeachment investigation

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:35.660
and he imposed exactly
the kinds of burdens

00:17:35.660 --> 00:17:37.230
that he's saying a sitting president

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:38.540
should not have to deal with.

00:17:38.540 --> 00:17:41.620
So, let's think like a
lawyer about this position.

00:17:41.620 --> 00:17:43.680
I think the Republicans would argue

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:45.900
that the Supreme Court
is supposed to decide

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:48.160
what is mandated by the Constitution

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:52.160
and they should not let their
individual policy preferences

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:53.740
guide their decision making.

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:54.800
A Supreme Court Justice

00:17:54.800 --> 00:17:57.430
is supposed to interpret the Constitution

00:17:57.430 --> 00:17:59.910
and not make policy by fiat,

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:01.820
and I think the Republicans would argue

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:04.350
that this law review piece is advocating

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:06.280
that Congress pass a law

00:18:06.280 --> 00:18:09.720
and not that there is a
Constitutional mandate

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:11.480
for this particular policy position

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:12.780
and, in fact, Judge Kavanaugh

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:14.470
has made statements to that effect,

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:16.890
that he recognizes his policy preference

00:18:16.890 --> 00:18:18.910
is not mentioned in the Constitution

00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:21.350
and it may contradict
what's in the Constitution

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:24.630
and therefore his stance
as a sitting Justice

00:18:24.630 --> 00:18:27.090
on the Supreme Court is not informed

00:18:27.090 --> 00:18:28.880
by this particular law review article,

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:30.760
or the policies that he advocated.

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:32.850
I think the Democrats would argue

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:35.580
that while that's nice in theory,

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:38.740
isn't it funny how
often policy preferences

00:18:38.740 --> 00:18:41.270
line up with one's own personal theory

00:18:41.270 --> 00:18:44.140
of what the Constitution
mandates, what it says,

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:45.850
and what needs to be interpreted

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:47.710
as a result of the Constitution.

00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:50.260
Upon being elevated to the Supreme Court,

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:52.940
they suddenly find a textual reason

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:55.670
for their particular policy preference.

00:18:55.670 --> 00:18:58.040
The bottom line is, I don't
think we know for certain

00:18:58.040 --> 00:18:59.890
which way Judge Kavanaugh would rule

00:18:59.890 --> 00:19:01.830
if he was elevated to the Supreme Court.

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:04.880
You could argue both ways,
based on his prior statements.

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:07.480
So that takes us to question number four,

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:10.240
would Dr. Christine
Blasey Ford's allegations

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:12.270
of sexual assault hold up in court?

00:19:12.270 --> 00:19:14.210
And other wise, could they lead

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:18.040
to Judge Kavanaugh being
convicted of sexual assault?

00:19:18.040 --> 00:19:21.580
Now, criminal law has the
highest burden under the law.

00:19:21.580 --> 00:19:23.070
The prosecution, in other words,

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:24.650
the people making the claim,

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:28.100
must prove their case
beyond a reasonable doubt.

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:31.510
Now, the accused technically
has no burden at all.

00:19:31.510 --> 00:19:33.660
If the prosecution
doesn't meet their burden,

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:36.160
then the defendant should
not have to make any showing

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:38.030
in order to avoid conviction.

00:19:38.030 --> 00:19:40.600
So, to decide if the allegations
would hold up in court,

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:42.360
we need to look at the evidence.

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:43.460
Now, there is one statement

00:19:43.460 --> 00:19:45.040
that I see coming up in the news a lot,

00:19:45.040 --> 00:19:46.800
that I think we should talk about.

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:48.510
A lot of pundits have argued

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:51.330
that there is no evidence
of sexual assault.

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:53.600
One of my biggest pet peeves as a lawyer

00:19:53.600 --> 00:19:57.030
is when people refer to a
he said/she said scenario

00:19:57.030 --> 00:19:59.630
and refer to it as if
there is no evidence.

00:19:59.630 --> 00:20:01.870
Even if there is only he said/she said

00:20:01.870 --> 00:20:03.010
with no physical evidence,

00:20:03.010 --> 00:20:06.900
there is, in fact, evidence
in the form of testimony.

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:09.820
Imagine if you had a police
officer who testified

00:20:09.820 --> 00:20:12.830
that she saw a bank robber rob a bank.

00:20:12.830 --> 00:20:14.630
The bank robber could deny that

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:17.720
and technically that is a
he said/she said scenario.

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:19.340
But, I bet you would probably think

00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:21.610
the first-hand testimony
of the police officer

00:20:21.610 --> 00:20:23.870
was pretty strong evidence

00:20:23.870 --> 00:20:26.636
and, in fact, often you
get people talking about

00:20:26.636 --> 00:20:29.420
circumstantial versus direct evidence

00:20:29.420 --> 00:20:32.540
and this is another one of big pet peeves.

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:34.600
Circumstantial evidence is evidence

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:36.160
that relies on an inference

00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:39.550
in order to connect it
to a conclusion of fact,

00:20:39.550 --> 00:20:42.420
for example, DNA evidence
at the scene of a crime.

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:44.200
By contrast, direct evidence supports

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:46.590
the truth of an assertion directly

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:50.260
without the need for additional
evidence or inference.

00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:53.110
So, I don't mean to imply
that circumstantial evidence

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:55.460
is ever inherently bad or weak,

00:20:55.460 --> 00:20:56.810
often circumstantial evidence

00:20:56.810 --> 00:20:58.860
is the strongest evidence that there is,

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:00.690
but in this particular case,

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:03.010
we happen to have direct evidence,

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:05.940
we have Dr. Christine
Blasey Ford's testimony.

00:21:05.940 --> 00:21:07.850
Now, you may believe her testimony

00:21:07.850 --> 00:21:09.730
or you may not believe her testimony,

00:21:09.730 --> 00:21:11.870
but if this were a court of law

00:21:11.870 --> 00:21:14.936
that would still qualify
as particular evidence.

00:21:14.936 --> 00:21:17.710
It would be direct evidence in the form

00:21:17.710 --> 00:21:20.270
of her particular first-hand testimony

00:21:20.270 --> 00:21:23.410
of what happened to her in 1983.

00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:26.280
So, as a threshold
matter, there is evidence.

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:29.050
There is evidence on the side
of Dr. Christine Blasey Ford

00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:30.350
in the form of her testimony

00:21:30.350 --> 00:21:32.690
and there is evidence on
the side of Judge Kavanaugh

00:21:32.690 --> 00:21:34.870
in the form of his direct testimony

00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:36.470
and that is separate from the question

00:21:36.470 --> 00:21:39.130
of whether Dr. Ford's
testimony would meet the burden

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:40.740
of beyond a reasonable doubt.

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:44.330
I don't know how a jury would
come out on this question,

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:46.170
but as we discussed with perjury,

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:47.610
I'm not sure that whether something meets

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:49.230
the technical legal definition

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:52.040
and the hurdle of beyond
a reasonable doubt

00:21:52.040 --> 00:21:54.070
is really beside the point or not.

00:21:54.070 --> 00:21:56.280
So that brings me to question number five,

00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:59.930
should Judge Kavanaugh have
requested an investigation?

00:21:59.930 --> 00:22:01.930
During the hearing,
the Democratic senators

00:22:01.930 --> 00:22:04.830
asked a lot of questions
of Judge Kavanaugh

00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:06.875
asking if he would be in favor

00:22:06.875 --> 00:22:10.890
of a further FBI
investigation into his conduct

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:13.820
and what Dr. Ford alleges happened to her.

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:16.430
- So I'm gonna ask you one last time,

00:22:16.430 --> 00:22:19.140
are you willing to ask the White House

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:22.940
to authorize the FBI to
investigate the claims

00:22:22.940 --> 00:22:24.290
that have been made against you?

00:22:24.290 --> 00:22:26.310
- I'll do whatever the
Committee wants, of course--

00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:27.386
- [Senator Harris] And I've
heard you say that, but--

00:22:27.386 --> 00:22:28.680
- The witness statements--
- I've not heard you ask--

00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:29.880
I've not heard you answer

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:32.115
a very specific question
that's been asked,

00:22:32.115 --> 00:22:36.550
which is are you willing
to ask the White House

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:40.850
to conduct an investigation by the FBI

00:22:40.850 --> 00:22:42.520
to get to whatever you believe

00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:44.520
is the bottom of the allegations

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:46.573
that have been levied against you?

00:22:47.680 --> 00:22:51.310
- And whether he should have
requested an FBI investigation

00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:54.010
probably depends on your
definition of should.

00:22:54.010 --> 00:22:56.610
As an attorney, I can't
imagine a situation

00:22:56.610 --> 00:22:58.910
where I counsel a client to ask

00:22:58.910 --> 00:23:02.170
for a further investigation
of that client.

00:23:02.170 --> 00:23:04.430
Speaking as an attorney,
it's usually not a good idea

00:23:04.430 --> 00:23:07.940
to encourage an investigation
into your client

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:10.410
and just because you don't
have anything to hide

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:13.500
doesn't mean you have anything
you actively want to share.

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:15.490
But that particular legal advice

00:23:15.490 --> 00:23:17.810
would have no bearing
in this particular case

00:23:17.810 --> 00:23:19.700
as to whether calling for an investigation

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:21.330
would be a good PR move

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:24.650
or whether it is the
morally right thing to do.

00:23:24.650 --> 00:23:26.610
I'll leave that to the moral philosophers

00:23:26.610 --> 00:23:28.650
because this is not a legal question,

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:32.650
this is a political,
ethical, and moral question

00:23:32.650 --> 00:23:35.720
and, in general, you don't
wanna ask your lawyer

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:37.530
for philosophical advice.

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:39.370
So, do you agree?

00:23:39.370 --> 00:23:40.860
Leave your objections in the comments

00:23:40.860 --> 00:23:42.740
and let me know what other
pressing legal issues

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:44.010
I should cover next.

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:46.753
And as always, I see you in court.

